// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  // ── Chinese ──
  "chinese:hunt": "Every autumn from 1681 the Kangxi Emperor rode north beyond the Great Wall to the Mulan hunting grounds (木兰围场). Thousands of bannermen formed a vast ring through the forest and slowly drew it tight, driving deer, boar and tigers toward the emperor's arrows. It was sport, but also drill: the Manchu court feared that peace and city life would soften its riders. Kangxi was proud of his tally of tigers, and the hunt ended with feasts for the Mongol princes who came to watch.",
  "chinese:battle": "In 1561 the coast of Zhejiang burned under raids by the wokou pirates (倭寇). General Qi Jiguang (戚继光) had recruited miners and farmers from Yiwu and drilled them in the mandarin-duck formation (鸳鸯阵): twelve men moving as one, rattan shields in front, long bamboo spears with their branches left on to tangle the enemy's swords. At Taizhou they won nine battles in a row. For the villages along the shore it felt like the first time in years the sea could be watched without dread.",
  "chinese:gather": "Chinese tradition remembers Shennong (神农), the Divine Farmer, who tasted a hundred herbs (神农尝百草) to learn which would heal and which would kill, poisoning himself many times a day. Gathering in China has carried that spirit ever since: villagers on the hills of Hangzhou plucking the first tender Longjing tea leaves before the Qingming festival, children sent up the slopes for bamboo shoots, old women who knew which root eased a fever. Each basket was knowledge handed down, leaf by leaf.",
  "chinese:ritual": "At the winter solstice the Ming and Qing emperors left the Forbidden City for the Temple of Heaven (天坛), built under Yongle in 1420. Before dawn, in the cold, the Son of Heaven climbed the three white marble tiers of the Circular Mound Altar to offer jade, silk, a burnt calf and music to Heaven. Musicians played the slow, ancient court music (雅乐). The whole realm's harvest and peace for the coming year was felt to rest on this one man bowing correctly in the dark.",
  "chinese:rain": "From 1876 to 1879 the rain failed across northern China: the Dingwu Famine (丁戊奇荒), when perhaps nine to thirteen million people died. Magistrates led processions to the dragon king's temple, set his image out in the sun so he would feel the drought, and carried willow branches. Farmers watched the cracked fields and the empty sky every morning. When the rains at last returned, the people who had survived planted again, quietly, because that is what one does.",
  "chinese:flood": "Long ago, the story goes, the waters rose until they swallowed the land. Gun tried to dam them and failed. His son Yu (大禹治水) spent thirteen years digging channels instead, leading the rivers to the sea. Three times he passed the gate of his own home and did not go in, though once he heard his newborn son crying inside. The Chinese have told this for three thousand years, beside a Yellow River that still breaks its banks, as a model of what a leader owes the people.",
  "chinese:wind": "Between 1405 and 1433 Admiral Zheng He (郑和) led seven great treasure fleets across the Indian Ocean. At Liujiagang and Changle he set up stone tablets thanking Tianfei (天妃), the sea goddess Mazu, for saving them in storms: when the wind howled and the waves stood like mountains, the sailors cried out to her and a holy light appeared at the masthead, and the sea grew calm. The tablets read less like boasts than like the relieved prayers of men who had expected to drown.",
  "chinese:fire": "In 206 BC, after the fall of Qin, the rebel lord Xiang Yu (项羽) entered Xianyang and set fire to the palaces of the First Emperor. Sima Qian wrote that the fire burned for three months. The legendary Epang Palace (阿房宫) became a byword: centuries later the poet Du Mu wrote that the people of Chu lit one torch and it all turned to scorched earth, and warned that rulers who do not pity their people will in turn be pitied by those who come after.",
  "chinese:thunder": "In Chinese villages thunder was the voice of Leigong (雷公), the Duke of Thunder, with a bird's beak, wings and a hammer and chisel, and of his partner Dianmu (电母), the Mother of Lightning, whose mirror lit up the wicked so his strike would not miss. People said that one who was unfilial or wasted rice would be struck by heaven's thunder (天打雷劈). When a storm rolled over, children were told to finish their bowls and be kind to their grandparents.",
  "chinese:birth": "After a birth a Chinese mother kept the month of confinement (坐月子), shut away from wind and cold water and fed chicken soup with ginger. On the child's first birthday came the zhuazhou (抓周): brush, coins, a seal, an abacus and a toy sword were set on a tray, and the family leaned in to see what the baby grabbed. A scholar, a merchant, an official? Everyone laughed, but everyone also watched closely, because no one could know yet what this life would become.",
  "chinese:death": "When a parent died, a Chinese family wore undyed hemp (披麻戴孝) and wailed by the coffin. A Daoist or Buddhist priest chanted, paper money and paper houses were burned so the dead would not be poor in the other world, and on the seventh day (头七) the soul was thought to visit home one last time. Under Confucian rules a son mourned a parent for three years, leaving office if he held one. Grief was not hidden; it was a duty carried out with care.",

  // ── Shang ──
  "shang:hunt": "The Shang kings hunted constantly, and they asked the ancestors first. On ox shoulder blades and turtle shells (甲骨) the diviners carved questions: will the king hunt at this place today without disaster? Afterward they sometimes carved the result: so many deer, so many boar. One famous bone, from the last reign, records that the king caught a great wild rhinoceros (兕) and was given a gift. The hunt was royal pleasure, military practice and a test of whether heaven still favoured the house of Shang.",
  "shang:battle": "Around 1200 BC, oracle bones of King Wu Ding (武丁) record his consort Fu Hao (妇好) raising some thirteen thousand troops against the Qiang (羌) people in the west, the largest army mentioned anywhere in the bones. She led other campaigns too, against the Tu and the Yi. Bronze axes with her name were found in her tomb. The diviners asked again and again whether she would receive divine help. For the Shang, war was something the ancestors joined, and a general was also a priest.",
  "shang:gather": "In the loess valleys around Anyang the Shang grew millet, and every season the diviners asked the same anxious question: will we receive the harvest (受年)? Laborers called zhongren (众人) worked the fields together under royal officers, cutting the heavy heads of millet with stone and shell sickles. Part of the grain went into ale for the ancestors, who were believed to be hungry too. A good harvest meant the spirits were content, and the king's divinations were being answered well.",
  "shang:ritual": "In the royal temple at Yin the diviner drilled hollows into a cleaned turtle plastron, then pressed a heated bronze rod into them. The shell cracked with a sharp sound (卜 still looks like those cracks). The king read the lines himself: the king prognosticated, saying (王占曰) it will be auspicious. Questions covered everything: rain, war, harvest, toothache, dreams. Offerings of cattle, wine and sometimes captives were made to Di (帝) and the royal ancestors. It was solemn work: asking the dead to reveal tomorrow.",
  "shang:rain": "One of the best-known oracle bones from Anyang asks, on the day guimao: today, will it rain? Will the rain come from the west? From the east? From the north? From the south? Shang life depended on the rains reaching their millet fields, and the diviners carved the question to all four directions as if turning to look at each horizon. Many other bones record the king offering to the river and the mountain spirits, and simply waiting for clouds.",
  "shang:flood": "The Shang moved their capital several times, and later tradition blamed the Yellow River's floods. When King Pan Geng (盘庚) moved the court to Yin around 1300 BC, the people did not want to go. The Book of Documents preserves his speeches: he scolded, persuaded, and promised that the ancestors themselves wished the move. The new capital at Anyang lasted over two centuries. Behind the speeches one can still hear an ordinary people afraid of leaving their homes for higher ground.",
  "shang:wind": "The Shang believed the winds came from the four quarters, each with its own spirit and name. A famous bone lists them: the east is called Xi and its wind Xie; the south is Yin and its wind Kai, and so on (四方风). The wind was also thought to be a messenger of the high god Di, sometimes written with the same sign as the phoenix (凤). When a great storm blew, the diviners asked whether it would bring harm to the king.",
  "shang:fire": "In 1046 BC King Wu of Zhou defeated the last Shang king, Di Xin (帝辛), at the battle of Muye. Sima Qian tells that Di Xin fled back to his capital, climbed the Deer Terrace (鹿台), dressed in his jade-covered robes, and set himself on fire. Six hundred years of Shang rule ended in that blaze. The Zhou later called him a tyrant, but for the people of Shang it was the night their whole world of ancestors and oracles burned.",
  "shang:thunder": "To the Shang, rain, wind and thunder were commanded by Di (帝), the high god above the ancestors, and the diviners asked whether Di would order thunder in a given month. The ancestors could be asked to plead for good weather, but Di himself received few direct offerings; he was too high and too unpredictable. When thunder broke over the Anyang plain, the Shang heard the will of a power that even their kings could only question, never command.",
  "shang:birth": "The oracle bones preserve a real divination about a royal birth. On day jiashen the king asked: Fu Hao will give birth; will it be good (妇好娩，嘉)? King Wu Ding read the cracks and said that if she gave birth on a ding day it would be good. Weeks later the result was carved: it was not good, it was a girl. Fu Hao herself went on to lead armies. The bone shows both the danger of every birth and the hopes a family pressed onto it.",
  "shang:death": "In 1976 archaeologists at Anyang opened the untouched tomb of Fu Hao (妇好), who died around 1200 BC. She had been laid in a lacquered coffin with 468 bronzes, 755 jades, cowrie shells, ivory cups, sixteen attendants and six dogs. King Wu Ding kept making offerings and asking about her spirit after her death. Shang funerals were grand and frightening: the dead remained powerful ancestors, and they had to be given everything they would need.",

  // ── Shu ──
  "shu:hunt": "Imagined: in the hills above the Chengdu plain, Shu officers rode out with dogs and bows in late autumn, chasing deer through bamboo thickets. The hunt fed the garrison, but it was also practice for the mountain roads to the north, where Zhuge Liang's armies would have to march. The men talked of home in Jing province, lost to Wu, and wondered whether the Han dynasty they served would ever return to Chang'an.",
  "shu:battle": "In 219 at Mount Dingjun (定军山), Liu Bei's army fought for control of Hanzhong. Fa Zheng watched for the moment, and the old general Huang Zhong (黄忠), already past sixty, charged down from the heights with drums roaring. The Wei commander Xiahou Yuan was killed. Liu Bei took Hanzhong and proclaimed himself its king. For the men of Shu it proved that their small state, cut off behind the mountains, could still beat the north.",
  "shu:gather": "Shu was famous for its brocade (蜀锦), woven from silk raised along the Jin River in Chengdu, where the cloth was washed until its colors shone. Women gathered mulberry leaves at dawn to feed the silkworms. Zhuge Liang wrote that the state now relied on brocade alone to fund its armies against the enemy. Every basket of leaves from the orchards was, in a small way, part of the war to restore the Han.",
  "shu:ritual": "In the spring of 221, after news came that the last Han emperor had been deposed by Cao Pi, Liu Bei ascended an altar south of Chengdu at Wudan and offered a sacrifice to Heaven and Earth. His proclamation declared that the Han had not ended: he would carry on its mandate. For his followers this was a solemn act of faith, asking Heaven to recognize a dynasty that by now held only one mountain-ringed province.",
  "shu:rain": "In 230 the Wei general Cao Zhen marched against Shu through the Ziwu valley. Shu could only fortify the passes and wait. Then the rain began and did not stop for more than thirty days. The plank roads clinging to the cliffs washed away, and the Wei army was ordered home. In Hanzhong the defenders, who had braced for a desperate fight, watched the clouds and felt the mountains and the sky had taken their side.",
  "shu:flood": "In the autumn of 219 Guan Yu (关羽) besieged Fancheng on the Han River. Heavy rains swelled the river until it burst its banks, and the seven armies under Yu Jin (于禁), camped on low ground, were swamped (水淹七军). Guan Yu's men came by boat; Yu Jin surrendered, and Pang De, who refused to, was executed. For a few weeks all of central China trembled. Within months Guan Yu himself would be killed, but the flood remained his greatest moment.",
  "shu:wind": "Before the battle of Red Cliffs in 208, the histories say only that a southeast wind blew, carrying Huang Gai's fire ships into Cao Cao's fleet. The later Romance of the Three Kingdoms gives the wind to Shu: Zhuge Liang climbs an altar on the south bank, unbinds his hair, and borrows the east wind (借东风) through his arts. The legend shows how the people of Shu wanted to remember their strategist: as a man who could talk to the sky.",
  "shu:fire": "In 222 Liu Bei invaded Wu to avenge Guan Yu. His army camped in a chain of forts stretching hundreds of li through the forests at Yiling (夷陵) in the summer heat. The young Wu commander Lu Xun waited, then sent men with torches. The camps burned one after another. Liu Bei fled to Baidicheng in shame and never returned to Chengdu. Shu would remember that fire as the night it lost a generation of soldiers.",
  "shu:thunder": "While Liu Bei was a guest of Cao Cao, Cao said to him over wine: the only heroes in the world are you and I. Liu Bei was so startled he dropped his chopsticks. Later tellings add that at that very moment thunder cracked, and Liu Bei calmly blamed his fright on it (煮酒论英雄). Whether or not the thunder was real, the story shows how dangerously Liu Bei had to live, hiding his ambition under the storm.",
  "shu:birth": "Liu Shan (刘禅), called A Dou, was born in 207 to Liu Bei, who was nearly fifty. The next year at Changban, as Cao Cao's cavalry overran the refugees, Zhao Yun carried the infant through the chaos to safety. The whole hope of a dynasty rested on that baby. He reigned for forty years, but in 263 he surrendered to Wei, and later said he was too happy in Luoyang to miss Shu. No one at his birth could have guessed either ending.",
  "shu:death": "Zhuge Liang (诸葛亮) died in camp at Wuzhang Plains in 234, during his last campaign. He had asked to be buried simply at Mount Dingjun in Hanzhong, in a tomb only big enough for the coffin, in his ordinary clothes, with no precious objects. The army withdrew in silence. Ordinary people in Shu began to offer to him privately in the fields, and in time the court allowed shrines. He is still mourned in Chengdu today.",

  // ── Wei ──
  "wei:hunt": "Cao Pi (曹丕), first emperor of Wei, loved hunting and wrote about it with pride. Once he rode out with his son Cao Rui and shot a mother deer. He told the boy to shoot the fawn. Cao Rui lowered his bow and wept: Your Majesty has already killed its mother; I cannot bear to kill the child. Cao Pi put down his own bow, moved, and from then on he thought of the boy as his heir.",
  "wei:battle": "In the winter of 208 Cao Cao (曹操) brought a vast northern army down the Yangtze, sure that the south would fall. At Red Cliffs (赤壁) his men, unused to boats and stricken with disease, were scattered by the fire ships of Zhou Yu and Huang Gai. Cao Cao retreated through the marshes on the Huarong road, where the mud was so deep that weak soldiers were trampled laying grass for the horses. The dream of reuniting China in one stroke ended there.",
  "wei:gather": "After years of civil war the north was empty and starving; soldiers ate mulberries and even each other. In 196 Cao Cao accepted Zao Zhi's plan for military farms (屯田): refugees and soldiers were settled on abandoned land near Xuchang and given oxen and seed. The first year brought in a million hu of grain. Gathering that harvest was the real foundation of Wei: armies that could eat could fight, and families who could eat could stay.",
  "wei:ritual": "In the tenth month of 220 the last Han emperor, Xian, handed the throne to Cao Pi. At Fanyang a raised altar (受禅台) was built. Cao Pi climbed it before the officials and the troops, received the seal, and burned offerings to Heaven to announce that the mandate had passed to Wei. He is said to have remarked afterward: now I know what happened between Shun and Yu. Stone stelae recording the ceremony still stand at the site.",
  "wei:rain": "In 230 the Wei regent Cao Zhen (曹真) marched toward Shu through the Ziwu valley, aiming to end the war in the west. Then the rain came: over thirty days without pause. The plank roads along the cliffs collapsed, supplies could not move, and the officials at court begged the emperor to recall the army. The soldiers sat in sodden camps watching the sky. The campaign was abandoned, and Cao Zhen fell ill and died the next year.",
  "wei:flood": "In 219 the general Yu Jin (于禁) camped with seven armies outside Fancheng. Autumn rains swelled the Han River until it broke its banks, and his men were trapped on mounds of earth as the water rose. Guan Yu's boats came for them. Yu Jin surrendered after thirty years of loyal service. His comrade Pang De (庞德) refused to kneel and was executed. Cao Cao said he had known Yu Jin for thirty years and never expected that.",
  "wei:wind": "In 225 Cao Pi led a huge fleet to the Yangtze at Guangling to invade Wu. Winter storms whipped the river into great waves, ice formed, and his warships could not cross. Standing on the bank looking south, he sighed: Heaven itself made this river to divide north and south (固天所以限南北也). He turned back. The storm, as much as Wu's armies, kept China divided for another half century.",
  "wei:fire": "In 200 at Guandu (官渡), Cao Cao's army was outnumbered by Yuan Shao's and running out of food. Then a defector, Xu You, told him where Yuan Shao's grain was stored: at Wuchao. Cao Cao himself led five thousand men by night, carrying Yuan banners and bundles of firewood, and burned the depot. Yuan Shao's army collapsed. The fire that night decided who would rule northern China.",
  "wei:thunder": "Imagined: a summer storm over Ye, Cao Cao's capital. On the Bronze Sparrow Terrace (铜雀台) the poets of the Jian'an circle paused their verses as lightning lit the walls and thunder rolled across the plain. Officials whispered about omens: was Heaven pleased with the house of Cao, or warning it? In the barracks the soldiers simply covered their heads and waited for the rain to pass, as they always had.",
  "wei:birth": "Cao Chong (曹冲), born around 196, was the child Cao Cao loved most. At about five, when Sun Quan sent an elephant, the boy weighed it by marking how low it set a boat in the water, then loading the boat with stones to the same mark (曹冲称象). Cao Cao hoped to make him heir. In 208 Cao Chong fell ill and died at twelve. Cao Cao told his other sons: this is my misfortune, but your good fortune.",
  "wei:death": "Before his death in 220 Cao Cao left a will: bury me in the clothes of the season, put no gold, jade or treasure in my tomb, and let the troops not leave their posts to mourn. The world was still at war and he did not want resources wasted. In 2009 archaeologists in Anyang identified his tomb, Gaoling, which indeed held modest goods: stone tablets naming his weapons, and the bones of a man of about sixty.",

  // ── Wu ──
  "wu:hunt": "Sun Quan (孙权), ruler of Wu, loved to ride out and shoot tigers. Once a tiger leaped up and clawed at his saddle. His old minister Zhang Zhao rebuked him sharply: a ruler should command heroes, not race wild beasts. Sun Quan apologized, but could not give it up; he had a special carriage built with a shooting window so he could hunt tigers from inside it. His brother Sun Ce had been killed while hunting alone, and the court never forgot.",
  "wu:battle": "In the winter of 208 Cao Cao's great northern fleet came down the Yangtze. Many in Wu urged surrender. Sun Quan drew his sword, cut the corner off his desk, and said anyone who mentioned surrender again would end like the desk. Zhou Yu (周瑜), just thirty-three, led the allied fleet with Liu Bei at Red Cliffs (赤壁). Huang Gai's burning ships crashed into the chained enemy fleet. For Wu it was the day the south proved it could stand alone.",
  "wu:gather": "Imagined: in the wet fields of Jiangdong, families of the Wu kingdom transplanted rice and waded the paddies at harvest, while the women raised silkworms through the warm months. The northerners had fled here from war in their tens of thousands, and the old southerners and the newcomers learned to farm side by side. Every boat of rice sent up to Jianye fed the soldiers who guarded the long river line.",
  "wu:ritual": "In 229 Sun Quan finally declared himself emperor at Wuchang. Officials reported auspicious signs: a yellow dragon and phoenixes had been seen, and he named his era Huanglong (黄龙), Yellow Dragon. On the altar he offered to Heaven and announced that the mandate had come to the south as well. For a people long dismissed by northerners as marsh-dwellers, it was a solemn claim that Heaven saw them too.",
  "wu:rain": "In 222 Liu Bei's avenging army camped deep in Wu territory. The young commander Lu Xun (陆逊) refused to fight. For months his generals, many older and nobler than he, raged at him for cowardice. Lu Xun simply waited, through spring rains into summer heat, until the Shu soldiers grew tired and careless in their forest camps. He was waiting for the world to change, and when it did, he struck.",
  "wu:flood": "Imagined: one summer the Yangtze rose for weeks, brown and fast, flooding the low fields around Jianye. Wu farmers dragged their boats to the doorsteps and carried grandparents and rice jars up to the dykes. Soldiers were sent from the garrisons to fill baskets with earth. When the water fell, they planted again in the fresh silt, saying, as Jiangdong people always had, that the river takes, and the river gives.",
  "wu:wind": "Imagined: a summer storm caught a Wu fleet on the Yangtze near Wuchang. The great tower ships (楼船) heeled as the gale tore at their sails; oarsmen drove for the shore while others prayed to the river spirits. Wu ruled by the water, and its sailors knew how quickly the river could turn on them. When the wind dropped, they counted the missing boats and made offerings at the riverside shrine.",
  "wu:fire": "In the summer of 222 Liu Bei's army stretched its wooden camps along hundreds of li of forested valleys at Yiling (夷陵). Lu Xun (陆逊), who had waited for months, ordered each soldier to carry a bundle of reeds and set fire to the camps. The fire leapt from fort to fort. Over forty Shu camps fell and Liu Bei fled. Lu Xun, the young scholar mocked by older generals, became the hero of Wu.",
  "wu:thunder": "Imagined: in the hot summers of Jiangdong, storms build fast over the Yangtze and break in the afternoon. Wu villagers hurried the buffalo home, closed the shutters and lit incense before the thunder god's image. Sailors tied up their boats. Children were told that the thunder searched for wrongdoers and that the good had nothing to fear, though the grown-ups looked up at the sky a little nervously all the same.",
  "wu:birth": "The Records of the Three Kingdoms says that when Lady Wu, wife of the warrior Sun Jian, was pregnant with her first son Sun Ce, she dreamed the moon entered her body; when pregnant with Sun Quan (孙权), she dreamed of the sun. Sun Jian said the sun and moon are the essence of yin and yang, and his descendants would rise high. Both sons grew to rule the south, and Sun Quan founded the state of Wu.",
  "wu:death": "In 200 Sun Ce (孙策), conqueror of the south, was ambushed while hunting and died of his wounds at twenty-five. On his deathbed he gave the seal to his eighteen-year-old brother Sun Quan: in leading the armies of Jiangdong you are not my equal, but in employing worthy men I am not yours. Sun Quan wept without stopping. Zhang Zhao told him that grief must wait; he dressed him, put him on a horse and had him review the troops.",

  // ── Zhu Xi's Legacy ──
  "zhu-xis-legacy:hunt": "Imagined: in the hills of Fujian under the Southern Song, a scholar's household went out in winter with nets and dogs after hares and pheasants. The young men had read in the Book of Rites that one must not hunt in spring, when animals bear young, nor surround a herd on all sides. Their teacher, a follower of Zhu Xi, reminded them that even in a hunt one investigates things (格物) and keeps a humane heart.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:battle": "In 1279 at Yamen (崖山), off the coast of Guangdong, the last Song fleet of about a thousand ships was chained together against the Mongol-led Yuan navy. After weeks of siege and thirst, the line broke. The minister Lu Xiufu (陆秀夫), a Confucian scholar raised on Zhu Xi's teaching of loyalty, took the eight-year-old emperor on his back and leapt into the sea. Many officials and families followed. Song scholars long remembered it as the death of a civilization.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:gather": "In 1171, after famine in his district of Wufu in Fujian, Zhu Xi (朱熹) set up a community granary (社仓). In good years the villagers gathered grain into it; in hungry spring months they borrowed it at low interest and repaid after harvest. It was Confucian ethics made practical: neighbours caring for neighbours without waiting for the state. Zhu Xi asked the court to copy it everywhere, and community granaries spread across China.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:ritual": "In 1195, as the minister Han Tuozhou persecuted the so-called false learning (伪学), Zhu Xi drafted a long memorial attacking him. His students begged him not to send it. Finally Zhu Xi took out the yarrow stalks and divined with the Book of Changes. The answer was the hexagram Dun (遯): retreat. He burned the draft in silence and took the name Dunweng, the Old Man Who Retreats. He treated divination not as magic but as a serious consultation with the patterns of Heaven.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:rain": "In 1179 Zhu Xi became prefect of Nankang, at the foot of Mount Lu, and the next year drought struck. As a magistrate he led prayers for rain, as custom required, but he also cut taxes, opened granaries, organized relief and sent reports pleading for aid. He wrote that a ruler must feel the people's hunger as his own. He and his officials walked the dry fields each day, waiting for clouds and doing what human hands could do meanwhile.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:flood": "Imagined: one summer under the Southern Song, a mountain torrent swept down a valley near Wuyi, where Zhu Xi had once taught. The villagers fled to the hillside with their ancestral tablets in their arms before anything else. Afterward the local scholars organized repairs to the dykes and distributed grain, reading aloud from their teacher's writings that true learning must show itself in helping one's community.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:wind": "Imagined: a typhoon blew in from the sea over the Fujian coast, tearing roofs from the houses at Jianyang where Song printers carved the woodblocks of Zhu Xi's commentaries. The printers hurried to cover the blocks with oiled cloth. Afterward the scholars walked the town, and one remarked that wind too is only qi in motion, following its principle (理), and that a calm heart can weather any storm.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:fire": "Lin'an, the Southern Song capital at Hangzhou, was a crowded city of wooden houses, and great fires struck it again and again. Watchtowers stood across the city, and fire brigades of soldiers rushed out with buckets, axes and ladders at the first smoke. Officials who followed Zhu Xi's teaching asked whether the fires were Heaven's warning about misgovernment, and urged the emperor to examine his conduct and relieve the homeless.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:thunder": "Zhu Xi's students once asked him what thunder is. He answered, as recorded in the Classified Conversations (朱子语类), that it is the clash of yin and yang qi: when yang is trapped and bursts out, there is thunder; it is like firing a gun. He did not deny spirits entirely, but he taught that the world follows principle (理) and can be understood. For his followers, a thunderstorm was awe-inspiring, but also something to study calmly.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:birth": "Zhu Xi was born in 1130 at Youxi in Fujian, while northern China was overrun by the Jurchen Jin and his family had fled south. Later followers said he had seven moles on his right cheek arranged like the Big Dipper. His father, Zhu Song, was a minor official who opposed peace with the Jin. No one then could know this child would shape Chinese, Korean and Japanese thought for seven hundred years.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:death": "Zhu Xi died in 1200 while his learning was still banned as false (庆元党禁). The court warned officials against gathering at his funeral, yet nearly a thousand people came to Jianyang to bury him according to the rites he had written down in his Family Rituals (朱子家礼). The poet Xin Qiji wrote in his eulogy that what never decays is a name handed down for ten thousand generations. Within decades his commentaries became the basis of the civil examinations.",

  // ── Japanese ──
  "japanese:hunt": "In 1193 Minamoto no Yoritomo, the new shogun, held a grand hunt at the foot of Mount Fuji (富士の巻狩り). Warriors drove deer and boar toward the archers for weeks, and his son Yoriie's first kill was celebrated as a sign from the gods. On the last night, the Soga brothers, Sukenari and Tokimune, slipped into the camp in the rain and killed Kudo Suketsune, who had murdered their father eighteen years earlier. The tale of their revenge is still performed on the kabuki stage.",
  "japanese:battle": "In 1185 the Taira and Minamoto fleets met at Dan-no-ura (壇ノ浦) in the strait of Shimonoseki. When the tide turned against them, the Taira saw that all was lost. The grandmother of the six-year-old emperor Antoku took him in her arms; he asked where they were going, and she told him there was a capital beneath the waves. They sank together. The Tale of the Heike begins with a temple bell ringing of the impermanence of all things.",
  "japanese:gather": "Each June in the rice villages of Japan came taue (田植え), the planting. Women called saotome, in matching kimono and straw hats, bent in a line through the flooded paddies, setting seedlings by hand while drummers and flute players on the banks played rice-planting songs (田植え歌) to keep the rhythm and please the god of the fields. At autumn harvest the first grain went to the shrine. Gathering rice was never only work: it was a festival with the gods.",
  "japanese:ritual": "At Ise Jingu (伊勢神宮), Japan's holiest shrine, the sanctuary of the sun goddess Amaterasu is rebuilt from fresh cypress every twenty years in the Shikinen Sengu (式年遷宮), a custom traced to 690. On the night of the transfer, priests in white carry the sacred mirror, hidden under silk screens, from the old shrine to the new in darkness and silence. It is a prayer that the land will be renewed, and a promise between generations.",
  "japanese:rain": "In 824, as drought parched the capital, the court asked two great monks to pray for rain at the Shinsen-en garden in Kyoto. Legend says the monk Shubin had trapped all the dragon gods in a jar, but Kukai (空海) found one who had escaped, the dragon queen Zennyo, and invited her from the Himalayas. Rain fell across Japan for three days. Farmers long held rain-calling rites (雨乞い) at village ponds, drums beating, waiting for the clouds.",
  "japanese:flood": "The Kamanashi River in Kai flooded almost every year, ruining the rice plains. In the 1540s the warlord Takeda Shingen began building a system of staggered levees, stone spurs and redirected channels that turned the floods aside. Villagers who settled behind it were excused from taxes in return for guarding it. Parts of the Shingen Levee (信玄堤) still protect Kofu today, and the people of Yamanashi still speak of him with gratitude.",
  "japanese:wind": "In the summer of 1281, more than four thousand ships of the Mongol-led Yuan fleet gathered off Hakata Bay. For weeks samurai had fought them from the stone wall built along the shore. Then, in mid-August, a great typhoon struck, sinking most of the fleet. Japanese temples and shrines had been praying for months, and the people called it the divine wind (神風, kamikaze): proof that the gods protected the land. Relief swept the country like the storm itself.",
  "japanese:fire": "On the eighteenth day of the first month of 1657, a fire started in Hongo in Edo and was driven by strong winds through a city dry from a winter without rain. Over three days the Meireki Fire (明暦の大火) burned most of the capital, including the main keep of Edo Castle, and killed perhaps a hundred thousand people. Legend blamed a cursed long-sleeved kimono (振袖火事) being burned at a temple. Edo rebuilt with firebreaks, wider streets and fire brigades.",
  "japanese:thunder": "Sugawara no Michizane, a great scholar exiled unjustly in 901, died in Dazaifu in 903. Soon his enemies began to die, and in 930 lightning struck the Seiryoden of the imperial palace, killing several courtiers. The court believed his angry spirit had become the thunder god. He was pardoned, promoted after death, and enshrined as Tenjin (天神), now the god of learning. People say kuwabara, kuwabara during storms, because lightning was said never to strike his old estate.",
  "japanese:birth": "In the fifth month of pregnancy, on a day of the dog (戌の日), a Japanese mother-to-be visited a shrine such as Suitengu in Tokyo and was wrapped in a long white sash (岩田帯), because dogs give birth easily and many pups survive. Childbirth was dangerous, and families prayed hard. After a month the baby was carried to the local shrine for its first visit (お宮参り), and the family asked the gods to watch over whatever life this child would lead.",
  "japanese:death": "Most Japanese funerals follow Buddhist custom. The family keeps an all-night vigil (通夜), a priest gives the dead a new Buddhist name (戒名), and the body is cremated. Then comes kotsuage (骨上げ): two relatives together lift the bones with long chopsticks into the urn, starting from the feet so the dead will not be upside down. Each summer at Obon the ancestors are welcomed home with lanterns and dances, then sent back with fires.",

  // ── Yamato ──
  "yamato:hunt": "The Kojiki tells that Emperor Yuryaku, in the fifth century, went hunting on Mount Katsuragi with his court dressed in blue robes and red cords. Across the valley came another procession exactly like his own. Angry, the emperor demanded its leader's name, and the stranger answered that he was Hitokotonushi (一言主), the god who settles good and bad with a single word. Awed, Yuryaku bowed, offered his sword, bow and the clothes of his men, and the god escorted him home.",
  "yamato:battle": "In 663 Yamato sent a great fleet to help restore the fallen kingdom of Baekje in Korea. At the mouth of the Baekgang river, called Hakusukinoe (白村江) in Japan, they met the Tang and Silla navies. The Nihon Shoki says the Yamato ships charged in disorder and were caught between the enemy lines; in a short time many drowned, and the river ran red. Fearing invasion, the court built stone fortresses and beacon fires across Kyushu.",
  "yamato:gather": "The first poem of the Man'yoshu (万葉集) is said to be by Emperor Yuryaku. He meets a young woman gathering spring greens on a hill: With a basket, a pretty basket, with a trowel, a pretty trowel, you maiden picking herbs on this hillside, tell me your house, tell me your name. Gathering young herbs in early spring was a seasonal custom in Yamato, and the poem catches its brightness: fresh shoots, open skies, a new year beginning.",
  "yamato:ritual": "The Chinese Records of Wei describe the land of Wa in the third century, ruled by the queen Himiko (卑弥呼), who served the way of the spirits and bewitched the people. Before any journey or undertaking, the Wa heated bones over fire and read the cracks to tell good fortune from bad. In Japan this became futomani (太占): the shoulder blade of a deer burned over cherry bark, its cracks asking the gods what would come.",
  "yamato:rain": "In 642 a severe drought struck Yamato. The Nihon Shoki says the powerful minister Soga no Emishi had Buddhist sutras read and incense burned, and only a light rain fell. Then Empress Kogyoku went to the riverhead at Minabuchi, knelt, bowed to the four directions and prayed to Heaven. Thunder rolled and heavy rain fell for five days, reaching the whole land. The farmers praised her as an empress of supreme virtue.",
  "yamato:flood": "The Nihon Shoki says Emperor Nintoku built the Mamuta embankment (茨田堤) against the floods of the Yodo River, but two gaps kept breaking. He dreamed that a god demanded two men be sacrificed. One man, Kowakubi, was thrown in. The other, Koromono-ko, threw two gourds into the water and said: if you are truly a god, sink these; if they float, you are false. The gourds floated, he lived, and the dyke held.",
  "yamato:wind": "Yamato's envoys to Tang China (遣唐使) crossed the sea in fragile ships, and many died in storms. The Chinese monk Jianzhen (鑑真, Ganjin) was invited to Japan in 742; five voyages failed to typhoons and shipwreck, and he went blind on the way. On his sixth attempt, in 753, he finally reached Japan and founded Toshodai-ji in Nara. The envoy Abe no Nakamaro, on the same fleet, was blown off course and never returned home.",
  "yamato:fire": "The Nihon Shoki records that in the fourth month of 670, in the middle of the night, the temple of Horyu-ji, founded by Prince Shotoku, caught fire and not a single building was left. Heavy rain and thunder followed. The temple was rebuilt in the following decades, and today its wooden halls are among the oldest surviving in the world, still carrying the devotion of the people who would not let it vanish.",
  "yamato:thunder": "The Nihon Ryoiki tells that Emperor Yuryaku once asked his attendant Sugaru (栖軽) to fetch the thunder god. Sugaru rode out with a red banner calling for the god to come in the emperor's name. Lightning fell on a hill, and Sugaru brought the thunder god back in a palanquin. The emperor, frightened by its brilliance, had it returned. The place was named Ikazuchi-no-oka, Thunder Hill, still in Asuka today.",
  "yamato:birth": "Legend in the Kojiki and Nihon Shoki says Empress Jingu was pregnant when she led an expedition across the sea to Korea. To delay the birth until her return, she tied stones to her waist. She came home and gave birth in Tsukushi to the future Emperor Ojin, later worshipped as the war god Hachiman. Her stones, the chinkaiseki (鎮懐石), were revered as charms for safe childbirth by Yamato mothers for centuries.",
  "yamato:death": "Yamato rulers were buried in great keyhole-shaped mounds (前方後円墳) like the tomb attributed to Emperor Nintoku, among the largest graves on earth. Their slopes were lined with clay figures called haniwa (埴輪). The Nihon Shoki says that when Empress Hibasu-hime died, the craftsman Nomi no Sukune proposed clay figures instead of burying servants alive, and the emperor agreed. Rows of haniwa warriors, horses and houses watched over the dead.",

  // ── Sengoku Daimyo ──
  "sengoku-daimyo:hunt": "Oda Nobunaga loved falconry (鷹狩り). The Shincho Koki chronicle records him riding out again and again with his hawks, and he once held a hunt with hundreds of falconers. Hunting grounds also let a lord inspect his land, test his men and show the peasants his power. Tokugawa Ieyasu, who also adored falconry, said it kept the body strong and let him see how the farmers lived. For the warlords, the hunt was rest and strategy in one.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:battle": "In 1561, at the fourth battle of Kawanakajima (川中島), the armies of Takeda Shingen and Uesugi Kenshin met in morning fog. Legend says Kenshin rode alone into Shingen's headquarters and struck at him three times with his sword; Shingen, still seated, parried with his iron war fan. Both lost thousands that day. The two rivals fought five times over a decade and never decided anything, but the story of their duel was told for centuries.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:gather": "In 1588 Toyotomi Hideyoshi ordered the sword hunt (刀狩): every farmer in Japan must hand over swords, bows, spears and guns. The edict said the metal would be melted down for nails in the great Buddha he was building in Kyoto, so the farmers would be saved in this life and the next. In truth it ended a century in which peasants fought as warriors. Gathering weapons, Hideyoshi gathered the whole country under one hand.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:ritual": "Uesugi Kenshin believed himself an avatar of Bishamonten (毘沙門天), the Buddhist god of war, and flew a banner with the character 毘. Before battles he shut himself in the Bishamon hall at Kasugayama castle for days of prayer and fasting. Many daimyo also kept gunbaisha, military advisers who chose lucky days and directions by divination. Asking the gods before a battle was as natural as checking the weapons.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:rain": "In 1560 Imagawa Yoshimoto marched on Kyoto with an army of perhaps twenty-five thousand. Oda Nobunaga, with only a few thousand, prayed at Atsuta Shrine and then waited in the hills above Okehazama (桶狭間). Around midday a violent rain and hail storm broke over the valley. When it cleared, Nobunaga charged into Yoshimoto's resting camp, and Yoshimoto was killed. The world had changed in one storm.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:flood": "In 1582 Toyotomi Hideyoshi besieged Takamatsu castle in Bitchu, set in marshland. Instead of storming it, his men built a long earthen dam in about twelve days and diverted the rivers; the rainy season filled the plain, and the castle became an island (水攻め). To save his garrison, the commander Shimizu Muneharu rowed out in a small boat, performed a final dance, and took his own life before both armies.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:wind": "Imagined: in the eighth month a typhoon swept across the rice plains of a Sengoku domain just before harvest. The lord's retainers rode through the villages afterward counting flattened fields, and the castle granary was opened to the hungriest. The headmen begged for lower taxes. With enemies on every border, the daimyo knew that a ruined harvest could be as dangerous as a lost battle, and he sent rice south to buy peace.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:fire": "Before dawn on the second day of the sixth month of 1582, Akechi Mitsuhide's troops surrounded Honno-ji temple in Kyoto, where Oda Nobunaga was staying with a small guard. When told who the attackers were, Nobunaga is recorded to have said: it cannot be helped (是非に及ばず). He fought with bow and spear, then withdrew inside as the temple burned. His body was never found. The man who had almost united Japan vanished in the flames.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:thunder": "Tachibana Dosetsu, a retainer of the Otomo clan in Kyushu, was resting under a tree during a storm when lightning struck. He drew his sword Chidori and, legend says, cut the thunder god inside the bolt. He was left lame but alive, and his sword was renamed Raikiri (雷切), Lightning Cutter. In an age of endless war, the Sengoku warriors loved a story in which even the sky could be fought.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:birth": "In 1593 Lady Yodo gave birth to a son, Hiroi, later named Hideyori (豊臣秀頼). His father, Toyotomi Hideyoshi, was fifty-seven and had lost his first son. Overjoyed, he wrote affectionate letters to the baby and later forced his own nephew and heir to commit suicide. Before his death he made the great lords swear to protect the boy. In 1615 Osaka castle fell and Hideyori died at twenty-two, with all the hope placed on him.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:death": "Takeda Shingen died in 1573 during a campaign, perhaps of illness. His will, as the Koyo Gunkan records, ordered that his death be kept secret for three years, so his enemies would not take advantage. A double sat in his place, and letters went out under his seal. His body is said to have been sunk in Lake Suwa in armor, or buried quietly at Erin-ji. His retainers mourned in silence, guarding the secret.",

  // ── Koreans ──
  "koreans:hunt": "In 1404 King Taejong of Joseon, out hunting with hawks and hounds, fell from his horse while chasing a deer. As he got up, his first words to his attendants were: do not let the historian know. But the court historian had already heard, and the Annals of the Joseon Dynasty (조선왕조실록) record both the fall and the king's order not to record it. For Koreans the story became a proud symbol of honest history that even a king could not bend.",
  "koreans:battle": "In 1597 Admiral Yi Sun-sin (이순신), just restored to command after torture and demotion, had only thirteen ships left. He wrote to the king: your servant still has twelve ships. At the Myeongnyang strait (명량), where the tide roars like a river, he faced some 133 Japanese warships. He held his flagship in the current while the others hesitated, then the tide turned and the enemy line broke. Koreans still call it the miracle of Myeongnyang.",
  "koreans:gather": "In the deep mountains of Korea, ginseng hunters called simmani (심마니) searched for wild ginseng (산삼). They kept strict taboos: purifying themselves beforehand, sleeping apart, speaking a secret language, and praying to the mountain god (산신). When one finally spotted the red berries, he shouted Simbatda! (심봤다, I have seen the root), and the others knelt. They dug it out slowly with deer-horn picks so no hair root would break.",
  "koreans:ritual": "At Jongmyo (종묘) shrine in Seoul, the spirit tablets of the Joseon kings and queens are honored in the Jongmyo Jerye (종묘제례), a royal ancestral rite performed for six centuries. Descendants in ceremonial robes offer food and wine in strict order as court musicians play Jongmyo Jeryeak, slow and grave, with stone chimes and bronze bells. It is not mournful: it is a solemn conversation with ancestors, asking them to watch over the land.",
  "koreans:rain": "In 1441, during the reign of King Sejong (세종), Korea created a standard rain gauge (측우기): a bronze cylinder placed in every province to measure rainfall precisely, about two centuries before Europe. Kings still led rain-prayer ceremonies (기우제) during droughts, and Sejong himself fasted for his people. But the gauges let officials plan taxes and relief by actual measurement. It was a mix of prayer and science, both aimed at the farmers' fields.",
  "koreans:flood": "In July 1925 the Han River rose higher than anyone had ever seen in the Great Flood of Eulchuk (을축년 대홍수). Villages along the river were swept away, and hundreds died around Seoul. Families climbed onto rooftops; boats rowed through the streets of Yongsan. In the old district of Amsa-dong the flood uncovered the remains of a Neolithic village. Survivors long dated other events by it: before and after the year of the great flood.",
  "koreans:wind": "In 1653 a Dutch East India ship, the Sperwer, was wrecked by a storm off Jeju Island. Thirty-six sailors reached shore. The Korean authorities sent them to Seoul, where they were kept for thirteen years; Joseon did not let outsiders leave. In 1666 Hendrick Hamel and seven others escaped by boat to Japan, and his journal gave Europe its first detailed account of Korea. A storm opened a door both peoples had kept shut.",
  "koreans:fire": "On the night of 10 February 2008, an arsonist set fire to Sungnyemun (숭례문), the great south gate of Seoul built in 1398 and listed as National Treasure No. 1. Firefighters fought for hours, but the wooden upper pavilion collapsed live on television. Crowds gathered weeping and left flowers before the ruins. The gate was carefully rebuilt with traditional tools and reopened in 2013, but Koreans still remember the shock of that night.",
  "koreans:thunder": "Imagined: on a summer afternoon in a Joseon village, a sudden storm broke over the rice paddies. A grandmother called the children inside and told them that the thunder was the thunder god (뇌신) searching for those who had wronged their parents. The children sat very still while the rain drummed on the thatched roof, and the grandmother laughed and gave them roasted chestnuts when the thunder moved on.",
  "koreans:birth": "When a baby was born in a Korean house, a straw rope called geumjul (금줄) was hung across the gate: with red chili peppers and charcoal for a boy, pine sprigs and charcoal for a girl. It told neighbors not to enter for three weeks, keeping illness away. The family prayed to Samsin halmeoni (삼신할머니), the birth grandmother goddess. At a hundred days came baegil (백일), and at the first birthday the doljabi (돌잡이), when the child chooses its future from a table of objects.",
  "koreans:death": "In a Joseon Confucian funeral the mourners wore coarse hemp and wailed aigo, aigo (곡) at set times. The coffin was carried on a colorful bier called sangyeo (상여), decorated with paper flowers, while a lead singer rang a bell and sang the bier song (상엿소리) and the bearers answered in chorus: now I go, leaving this world. The dead were buried on a hillside chosen by geomancy, and the family performed rites (제사) on every anniversary.",

  // ── Choson (Gojoseon) ──
  "choson:hunt": "Imagined: in the forests of Liaodong and the northern peninsula, the people of Gojoseon hunted deer and boar with bows of wood and horn, some tipped with bronze. A young man's first kill was carried home and shared out among the families of the village. An elder offered its blood to the mountain spirit and spoke a word for the animal, so the forest would keep giving. Then they feasted under the stars.",
  "choson:battle": "In 109 BC Emperor Wu of Han sent armies by land and sea against Wiman Joseon (위만조선). King Ugeo held his walled capital of Wanggeom for nearly a year; the Han generals quarrelled and failed. But inside the city, ministers who wanted peace finally killed the king in 108 BC. Even then the minister Seonggi kept resisting until he too was killed. The city fell, and Han set up its commanderies. The kingdom ended not in the field but from within.",
  "choson:gather": "Imagined: hundreds of villagers gathered at a riverside in the Korean Bronze Age to raise a dolmen (고인돌) for a respected chief. They had split a massive capstone from a cliff using wooden wedges soaked in water, dragged it on log rollers with ropes, singing in rhythm to pull together, and slid it onto the upright stones over a ramp of earth. Korea holds tens of thousands of dolmens, among the most in the world.",
  "choson:ritual": "In the founding myth, Dangun Wanggeom (단군왕검) was both priest and king: dangun may mean the one who serves heaven, wanggeom the ruler. On Mani mountain on Ganghwa Island stands the stone altar Chamseongdan (참성단), where tradition says Dangun offered sacrifices to Heaven. Koreans still light a sacred flame there for national festivals and celebrate Gaecheonjeol (개천절), the day heaven opened, each October 3.",
  "choson:rain": "The Samguk Yusa tells that Hwanung (환웅), son of the heavenly ruler, descended to Mount Taebaek bringing three thousand followers and three lords: the Earl of Wind (풍백), the Master of Rain (우사) and the Master of Clouds (운사). Through them he governed grain, life, sickness and justice. For a farming people, the founding of the nation began with the gods of weather themselves coming down to share the waiting for rain.",
  "choson:flood": "Imagined: a summer of heavy rain swelled the Taedong River until it broke into the fields around the Gojoseon settlements. The villagers carried their pottery, seed grain and bronze daggers up to the hill fort and sheltered there for days. When the water receded, the elders gathered at the dolmen of the old chief to offer thanks that no one had drowned, and the families went down together to rebuild their pit houses.",
  "choson:wind": "Imagined: a great autumn storm swept down from the steppe over the plains of Liaodong. In the Gojoseon villages the wind tore thatch from the pit houses and flattened the millet. The people believed it was the Earl of Wind (풍백), who had descended with Hwanung, reminding them of heaven's power. The shaman hung offerings on the sacred tree at the village edge, and everyone waited for the sky to be calm again.",
  "choson:fire": "Imagined: in the last days of Wanggeom in 108 BC, fires were lit along the walls as Han armies pressed the siege. Inside, families buried bronze mirrors and daggers beneath their floors, hoping to return for them. Two thousand years later archaeologists would find such hoards. The people of Joseon watched the smoke rise over their capital, unsure whether surrender would bring peace or slavery.",
  "choson:thunder": "Imagined: a summer storm crashed over the Gojoseon heartland, and lightning split an old oak on the ridge where the ancestors were buried. The villagers gathered in fear: was Heaven, the realm of Hwanin, angry with them? The shaman danced with bells and a bronze mirror, flashing it toward the sky, and offered millet and wine. By morning the storm had passed, and the split oak was bound with straw rope as a sacred tree.",
  "choson:birth": "The Samguk Yusa tells that a bear and a tiger begged Hwanung to become human. He gave them mugwort and twenty cloves of garlic and told them to stay out of the sunlight for a hundred days. The tiger gave up; the bear endured and became a woman, Ungnyeo (웅녀). She prayed under the sandalwood tree for a child, and Hwanung married her. Their son was Dangun, who founded Joseon, traditionally in 2333 BC. A whole people was born of her patience.",
  "choson:death": "The people of Gojoseon and the Korean Bronze Age buried their dead under dolmens (고인돌) and in stone-cist graves, often with mandolin-shaped bronze daggers (비파형동검) and fine bronze mirrors. The dolmen fields of Gochang, Hwasun and Ganghwa are now a UNESCO World Heritage site. Dangun himself, the legend says, did not die but withdrew to the mountain at the age of 1908 and became its mountain god (산신).",

  // ── Mongols ──
  "mongols:hunt": "In early winter the Mongol army held the great battue hunt, the nerge. Juvaini describes it: the riders spread in a ring many days' march wide, then closed it slowly over weeks, commanded by flags and drums, until all the game was trapped in a circle of men. Any soldier who let an animal escape was punished. The khan shot first, then the princes, then the troops; the last animals were set free. It was a feast, a census of the steppe and a rehearsal for war.",
  "mongols:battle": "In April 1241 the Mongol army under Batu and Subutai met King Bela IV of Hungary at the Sajo river near Mohi. At dawn Batu's men forced the bridge with stone-throwing catapults while Subutai crossed secretly downstream. The Hungarians were penned in their wagon camp; the Mongols left a gap open in their ring, and as the enemy fled through it, they rode them down along the road. For the steppe riders it was proof that nothing in the west could stop them.",
  "mongols:gather": "In the spring of 1206 the Mongol clans gathered at the source of the Onon river for a great kurultai (assembly). The white standard with nine tails was raised, and Temujin was proclaimed Chinggis Khan, ruler of all the people of the felt walls, as the Secret History calls them. Feuding tribes became one nation. Every summer afterward the herders gathered again for horse races, wrestling and archery, the games that live on as Naadam.",
  "mongols:ritual": "The Franciscan William of Rubruck, at the court of Mongke Khan in 1254, saw that the khan did nothing without first consulting a sheep's shoulder blade. Diviners laid the bones in fire until they cracked; if the cracks ran straight lengthwise, the khan would act; if they ran crosswise, he would not. The Mongols worshipped Eternal Blue Heaven (Mongke Tengri) and spoke to it through shamans (boo), who beat drums and called the spirits.",
  "mongols:rain": "Mongols believed some shamans could command the weather with a magic bezoar called the jada stone. Chronicles say that in 1232 at Sanfeng mountain (三峰山), as Tolui's tired army faced a far larger Jin force, it began to snow and did not stop for three days; later accounts credited a weather-maker with his stone. The frozen Jin troops collapsed. For ordinary herders, waiting for rain meant watching the grass and praying that spring would come before the animals starved.",
  "mongols:wind": "In 1274 and again in 1281 Kublai Khan sent fleets of Mongols, Chinese and Koreans to conquer Japan. Both times storms came. In 1281, after weeks of fighting at Hakata Bay, a typhoon struck; ship after ship, many hastily built from river boats, was smashed together and sunk, and tens of thousands drowned or were left on the shore. For the Mongols, who had conquered from Korea to Hungary, it was a bitter lesson: the sea and the wind did not obey the khan.",
  "mongols:fire": "In 1220 Chinggis Khan took Bukhara. Ibn al-Athir and Juvaini tell that he rode into the great mosque, climbed the pulpit, and told the people: I am the punishment of God; if you had not committed great sins, God would not have sent me. The city of wooden houses then burned, and only the brick mosque and palaces were left. For the Mongols it was terror used as strategy; for Bukhara it was the end of a world.",
  "mongols:thunder": "European envoys were struck by how much the Mongols feared thunder. John of Plano Carpini (1246) and William of Rubruck (1254) wrote that in a storm the Mongols drove strangers out of their tents, wrapped themselves in black felt, and hid until it passed. Anyone killed by lightning, and his tent and goods, had to be purified by passing between two fires. Mongol law also forbade bathing in rivers or washing clothes in summer, lest it anger the thunder.",
  "mongols:birth": "The Secret History of the Mongols tells that Temujin was born by the Onon river, at Deluun Boldog, holding in his right fist a blood clot the size of a knucklebone. It was taken as a sign of a conqueror. His father Yesugei named him after a Tatar chief he had just captured. Some saw a great destiny; others, perhaps, a bloody one. No one could know yet that the boy would unite the steppe and build the largest land empire in history.",
  "mongols:death": "Chinggis Khan died in 1227 during his campaign against Xi Xia. His body was carried back to Mongolia and buried secretly near the sacred mountain Burkhan Khaldun. Marco Polo heard that the escort killed everyone they met on the way, and later tradition says horses were driven over the grave until it vanished and a forest grew over it. The Mongols honored him through the Eight White Tents (Ordos), where his spirit is still served today.",

  // ── Jurchens ──
  "jurchens:hunt": "The Liao emperors prized the gyrfalcon haidongqing (海东青), the swift white falcon of the Jurchen lands. Every year Liao envoys came demanding them, and also demanding to be entertained by Jurchen women. Jurchen hunters had to fight neighbouring tribes along the falcon road to the coast to catch the birds. Resentment grew year by year. The falcons that hunted swans for the Liao court helped spark the uprising that destroyed the Liao.",
  "jurchens:battle": "In 1115 the Jurchen chieftain Wanyan Aguda (完颜阿骨打), newly proclaimed emperor of Jin, faced the Liao emperor Tianzuo's huge army at Hubudagang (护步答冈). The Jin had perhaps twenty thousand horsemen; the Liao numbers were reported as hundreds of thousands. Aguda cut his face with a knife and told his men that they must fight to the death or be destroyed. The Jurchen cavalry charged the Liao centre and broke it. The Liao never recovered.",
  "jurchens:gather": "The Jurchen rivers yielded the pearls of the north (北珠), taken from mussels in the icy Songhua and Amur rivers, prized at the Liao and Song courts. Divers broke through the ice in late autumn to gather them, and the work killed many. Jurchens also gathered ginseng, sable furs and honey in the forests. Much of it went as tribute to their overlords, and the Jurchens bitterly remembered every load when the time came to rebel.",
  "jurchens:ritual": "A Song chronicle of the 1100s, the Sanchao Beimeng Huibian, records that among the Jurchens, shanman (珊蛮) is the Jurchen word for a female shaman, and that they could communicate with the spirits. It is one of the earliest written traces of the word shaman. Jurchen shamans beat drums and wore bells and mirrors, asking the spirits of heaven, mountains and ancestors about sickness, war and the future. The Manchu court kept these rites until 1912.",
  "jurchens:rain": "Imagined: in a dry summer in the Jin dynasty, Jurchen villagers in Manchuria watched their millet wilt under a cloudless sky. The shaman set up a willow pole in the courtyard, the women brought wine and cakes, and the drum beat late into the night as she called on the spirits of heaven. The men went out each dawn to look at the mountains in the north, waiting for the clouds that would tell them the gods had heard.",
  "jurchens:flood": "In 1194, under the Jin dynasty ruled by the Jurchens, the Yellow River burst its dykes at Yangwu and shifted its course south, into the Huai river system. Vast farmlands in the plains became swamp, and the river would not return north for over six centuries. Jin officials struggled to rebuild dykes while the empire also faced growing threats from the Mongol steppe. For the farmers there, it felt as if the world itself had moved.",
  "jurchens:wind": "Imagined: a blizzard wind howled across the forests of the upper Songhua as Jurchen hunters returned from a winter sable hunt. They dug into the snow, pressed close to their horses and dogs, and waited out the storm under hides. One elder sang an old song to the spirit of the north wind. When the gale finally dropped, they counted each man, found all alive, and rode on to the village where smoke rose from the kang stoves.",
  "jurchens:fire": "In 1127 the Jurchen Jin armies captured the Song capital Kaifeng in the Jingkang incident (靖康之变). They carried off the retired emperor Huizong, the emperor Qinzong, thousands of members of the court, and the imperial treasures and libraries, and parts of the great city burned. For the Jurchens it was a staggering triumph, barely a dozen years after their uprising; for the Chinese it became a byword for national humiliation.",
  "jurchens:thunder": "In 1232 the Mongols besieged the Jin capital at Kaifeng. The Jurchen defenders used a new weapon: the zhentianlei (震天雷), heaven-shaking thunder, iron shells packed with gunpowder, lowered from the walls on chains onto the Mongol sappers below. The roar, chroniclers wrote, could be heard for a hundred li, and men and armour were blown to pieces. The Jin dynasty fell two years later, but the thunder of that siege was remembered.",
  "jurchens:birth": "The Manchus, descendants of the Jurchens, told how three heavenly maidens bathed in the lake Bulhuri at the foot of Mount Bukuri. A magpie dropped a red fruit on the youngest, Fekulen, who swallowed it and became pregnant. Her son Bukuri Yongson was born able to speak, and became the ancestor of Nurhaci's clan, which founded the Qing. The magpie was revered ever after, and the story was told at the birth of every new hope.",
  "jurchens:death": "A Song account of the Jin, the Da Jin Guozhi, describes the old Jurchen funeral: the dead were buried without coffins, horses and goods were burned as offerings (烧饭), and the mourners slashed their foreheads with knives so that blood and tears ran down together, called sending blood tears. It was grief made visible, the loudest honor a warrior people could give. As the Jin adopted Chinese ways, many of these customs faded.",

  // ── Khitans ──
  "khitans:hunt": "The Liao emperors moved their court with the seasons in camps called nabo (捺钵). In spring at the lakes of Changchun, drummers flushed the swans, and the emperor loosed a gyrfalcon (海东青) to bring down the first one. The first swan was offered at the ancestral shrine and celebrated with the first-swan feast (头鹅宴), where nobles stuck its feathers in their hair. For the Khitans the hunt was the state itself on horseback.",
  "khitans:battle": "In 979 the Song emperor Taizong besieged Liao's southern capital, Yanjing (modern Beijing). The Khitan generals Yelu Xiuge and Yelu Xiezhen rode to its rescue, and at the Gaoliang river (高梁河) they crushed the Song army. Taizong, wounded by an arrow, fled south in a donkey cart. For the Khitans it was a famous victory; the Sixteen Prefectures would remain in their hands, and the Song never took them back.",
  "khitans:gather": "In 1112 the Liao emperor Tianzuo held his spring camp on the Songhua river, where the chiefs gathered for the first-fish feast (头鱼宴): fish speared through holes in the ice. When the wine was flowing, he ordered the Jurchen chieftains to dance. One by one they did, except for Wanyan Aguda, who stood and stared. The emperor wanted to kill him but was dissuaded. Three years later Aguda founded the Jin, which destroyed the Liao.",
  "khitans:ritual": "The Khitans told that a heavenly man riding a white horse and a heavenly woman driving a grey ox met at Mount Muye (木叶山), where two rivers join, and their eight sons became the eight Khitan tribes. At Muye the Liao emperors sacrificed a white horse and a grey ox to heaven and earth, and to the ancestors, in the mountain-worship rite. The emperor asked the spirits for victories, good pasture and many children.",
  "khitans:rain": "In times of drought the Liao court held the sese rite (瑟瑟仪) to pray for rain. Willow branches were set up in the ground, and the emperor and nobles rode by shooting at them with arrows. Those who missed were punished by having to give wine to those who hit. If rain fell within three days, the officials received rewards. It was a horseman's prayer: drawing a bow to call down water on the grass.",
  "khitans:wind": "Imagined: a spring dust storm rolled out of the Gobi over the Liao camps. The yellow wind darkened the sky at noon; the court's felt tents shook and the herds turned their backs to the gale. Khitan herders tied the young lambs inside the gers and waited, cloths over their mouths. When it passed, they walked the pastures to find the scattered sheep, and the shamans burned juniper to cleanse the camp.",
  "khitans:fire": "Imagined: at the Liao winter camp a lamp toppled in the night and fire ran through the felt tents. The Khitan guards beat at it with hides and drove the horses clear while families dragged out their children and saddles. At dawn the shaman scattered ash and spoke to the spirit of fire, which Khitans honored with offerings of food burned at the hearth, asking it to stay inside the stove where it belonged.",
  "khitans:thunder": "Imagined: on the open grasslands of the Liao, summer thunderstorms rolled in fast and lightning had nowhere to strike but horses and riders. When a storm approached, Khitan herders galloped the herds into low ground and lay flat. A horse killed by lightning was left where it fell as belonging to heaven. Afterward the families offered a white sheep to the sky and thanked it for sparing them.",
  "khitans:birth": "The History of Liao says that before Yelu Abaoji (耶律阿保机) was born in 872, his mother dreamed that the sun fell into her bosom. At his birth the tent was filled with a strange light and fragrance, and his body was like a three-year-old's; he could crawl at once. His grandmother feared for him in a time of clan feuds and hid him, rubbing his face with soot. He grew up to unite the Khitans and found the Liao dynasty in 907.",
  "khitans:death": "When Abaoji died in 926, the custom was for loyal followers to follow him in death. His widow, Empress Shulu Ping (述律平), sent many officials to the tomb. One of them, Zhao Siwen, replied: no one was closer to the emperor than you. She answered that her children were young and the state needed her, then cut off her own right hand and had it buried with him. She was called the Empress of the Severed Wrist. Older Khitan custom exposed the dead on mountain trees.",
});
